Below is the proof. I work entirely in reasoning and output it here in the TUI as required.

---

**Proof.** Suppose for contradiction that both red and green points exist.

**Lemma 1.** *If one color has no noncollinear triple (all its points collinear, or at most two of them), then every point of the plane has the other color.*

*Proof of Lemma 1.* Suppose green has no noncollinear triple, so all green points lie on a single line ℓ (or there are at most two green points; take ℓ through them, arbitrary if there is at most one). Then every point off ℓ is red, and red certainly has a noncollinear triple. Let P be any point of the plane and consider a circle centered at P with positive radius. It contains infinitely many points, of which at most two lie on ℓ (or at most two are green), so at least three points on it are red. Three distinct points on a circle are noncollinear and have circumcenter P, so by the coloring condition P is red. Since P was arbitrary, all points are red. ∎

By Lemma 1, since both colors appear, **both** red and green must possess noncollinear triples.

**Lemma 2.** *Suppose a color (say red) has a noncollinear triple with circumcenter O and circumradius r > 0. Then every point X with |OX| > r/2 is red, except possibly for points on at most two fixed lines or at most two fixed points.*

*Proof of Lemma 2.* Let Γ be the circle centered at O of radius r through the red triple. If three points of Γ were green, they would be noncollinear (three distinct points on a circle) with circumcenter O, forcing O green — contradicting that O is red. Hence at most two points of Γ are green; call them (if they exist) g₁, g₂.

For a red point P ∈ Γ, set (with O as origin) L_P = {X : X·P = r²/2}; this is the tangent line to the circle Γ′ (center O, radius r/2) at T_P = P/2. We claim L_P \ {T_P} is red except possibly where it meets L_{g₁} or L_{g₂}. Indeed, for any red Q ∈ Γ with Q ≠ P and Q ≠ −P, the points O, P, Q are red and noncollinear (O is not on line PQ since P, Q are not antipodal), so their circumcenter is red. Placing O at the origin and P = (r, 0), the circumcenter of O, P, Q with Q = (r cos θ, r sin θ) is

  (r/2, (r/2) tan(θ/2)),

which, as θ ranges over (0, 2π) \ {π}, traces out the entire line x = r/2 minus the point (r/2, 0) = T_P. Thus L_P \ {T_P} is covered by red circumcenters, except for the values Q = g₁, g₂ (at most two green points); the corresponding excluded circumcenters are circumcenters of the non-monochromatic triple (O, P, gᵢ), and each lies on the perpendicular bisector of Ogᵢ, namely L_{gᵢ}. So the only possibly non-red points of L_P \ {T_P} lie on L_{g₁} ∪ L_{g₂}.

Now take any X with |OX| > r/2. It lies on exactly two tangent lines to Γ′. If both are L_P for red P ∈ Γ, then X (being strictly outside Γ′, hence not a tangent point T_P) is red. The only tangent lines not of this form are L_{g₁}, L_{g₂} (at most two lines). Therefore every point with |OX| > r/2 is red, except possibly points on L_{g₁} ∪ L_{g₂} (at most two lines) or the points g₁, g₂ themselves (at most two points). ∎

**Conclusion.** Apply Lemma 2 to red (circumcenter O_R, radius r_R) and to green (circumcenter O_G, radius r_G), and consider

  R = {X : |O_R X| > r_R/2 and |O_G X| > r_G/2},

the exterior of two closed disks — an open, unbounded, nonempty region. Within R, every point is red except possibly on ≤ 2 lines or at ≤ 2 points, and every point is green except possibly on ≤ 2 lines or at ≤ 2 points. Hence every point of R is both red and green except possibly on ≤ 4 lines or at ≤ 4 points. An open region of the plane cannot be covered by finitely many lines and points, so R contains a point that is simultaneously red and green — a contradiction.

Therefore both colors cannot appear; all points of the plane are the same color. ∎

